DEVELOPMENT --------------------------------------------------------

Handling duplicate names

  Some folks would like rsync to be deterministic in how it handles
  duplicate names that come from mering multiple source directories
  into a single destination directory; e.g. the last name wins.  We
  could do this by switching our sort algorithm to one that will
  guarantee that the names won't be reordered.  Alternately, we could
  assign an ever-increasing number to each item as we insert it into
  the list and then make sure that we leave the largest number when
  cleaning the file list (see clean_flist()).  Another solution would
  be to add a hash table, and thus never put any duplicate names into
  the file list (and bump the protocol to handle this).

                      --          --


Allow skipping MD4 file_sum2002/04/08

  If we're doing a local transfer, or using -W, then perhaps don't
  send the file checksum.  If we're doing a local transfer, then
  calculating MD4 checksums uses 90% of CPU and is unlikely to be
  useful.

  We should not allow it to be disabled separately from -W, though
  as it is the only thing that lets us know when the rsync algorithm
  got out of sync and messed the file up (i.e. if the basis file
  changed between checksum generation and reception).

                      --          --


loadparm.c: change lp_* return value to const char*

  The lp_*() helper that expands % tokens currently returns a non-const
  char*.  The return type should be changed to const char* at some
  point to make the API safer.  (See loadparm.c.)

                      --          --


options.c: Call poptReadDefaultConfig; handle errors

  The popt context creation should call poptReadDefaultConfig so that
  per-user and system-wide popt configuration files are honoured.
  Handle and report any errors returned.  (See options.c.)

                      --          --


options.c: -A flag should be ignored with a warning when ACLs are unsupported

  When rsync is built without ACL support, passing -A currently produces
  a hard error.  It should instead emit a warning and take countermeasures
  (restrict group/other access), matching the behaviour of other
  unsupported preservation flags.  (See options.c.)

                      --          --


clientserver.c: Show the bind address in daemon startup log message

  The "rsyncd version X starting, listening on port Y" log line does not
  include the bind address when one is configured.  It would be more
  informative to include it, e.g. via getnameinfo on the local address.
  (See clientserver.c.)

                      --          --


generator.c: Indicate '+' after permissions if the entry has an ACL

  The verbose itemize-changes output currently has no marker to show
  that a file carries an ACL.  A '+' suffix (similar to ls -l) would be
  useful.  (See generator.c.)

                      --          --


receiver.c: Integrate gen-reading I/O into perform_io()

  recv_files() currently calls io_flush() as a workaround to drive the
  generator-to-receiver data flow.  The proper fix is to fold that flow
  into perform_io() so the flush hack is no longer needed.
  (See receiver.c.)

                      --          --


io.c: Tune active_filecnt/active_bytecnt limits in perform_io()

  The hard-coded limits (10/50 files, 128 KB byte threshold) in the
  active-file throttle inside perform_io() were chosen without
  measurement.  They may benefit from tuning or becoming configurable.
  (See io.c.)

                      --          --


io.c: Avoid sleeping after each write

  The current code sleeps briefly after each write to throttle output.
  A non-blocking or event-driven approach would be cleaner and more
  efficient.  (See io.c.)

                      --          --


compat.c: Allow the xattr checksum algorithm to be configurable

  The algorithm used for xattr checksums is currently fixed at parse
  time and cannot be overridden.  The size of the digest is also
  constrained by xattr abbreviation limits.  In the future it would be
  nice to allow the algorithm to be chosen somehow.  (See compat.c.)

                      --          --


tls.c: Escape special characters in filenames in tls output

  The tls listing tool prints filenames verbatim.  Special characters
  (whitespace, control characters) in names could confuse output
  parsers.  Consider escaping them.  (See tls.c.)

                      --          --


clientname.c: Investigate whether rsync can continue after getpeername() failure

  When getpeername() fails on a connected socket, rsync calls
  exit_cleanup() immediately.  It is worth investigating whether a
  graceful fallback (e.g. treating the peer as anonymous) is safe and
  useful instead.  (See clientname.c.)

                      --          --


authenticate.c: Replace deprecated getpass() with a more portable alternative

  getpass() is deprecated: it may truncate passwords on some systems
  and is not in the LSB.  Replace it with a portable implementation,
  e.g. using getpassphrase() on Solaris or a custom terminal read.
  (See authenticate.c.)

                      --          --


log.c: Find a way to report log_init() errors back to the user

  When opening the log file fails inside log_init(), the error is
  currently swallowed because there is no established channel to send
  it over yet.  A mechanism to surface this error to the operator
  would be helpful.  (See log.c.)

                      --          --


util1.c: kill_all() should verify pids belong to our children before killing

  kill_all() iterates a pid array and sends a signal to each entry.
  If a pid has been recycled by another process the signal goes to the
  wrong target.  The array should be pruned as children are waited on,
  or process groups should be used.  (See util1.c.)

                      --          --


xattrs.c: --dry-run should compute the correct return value

  set_xattr() returns 1 unconditionally when --dry-run is active,
  without computing whether the xattr would actually have changed.
  This means the caller may report a spurious change.  The correct
  value should be computed even in dry-run mode.  (See xattrs.c.)

                      --          --


main.c: Log a proper message when a child process exits due to a signal

  wait_process_with_flush() detects that a child exited on a signal
  but does not log which signal or which child was affected.  A
  descriptive log message would make daemon post-mortems much easier.
  (See main.c.)

                      --          --


main.c: Exit more gracefully when the send file list is empty

  When the sender produces an empty file list, rsync currently calls
  exit_cleanup(0) directly.  A more controlled shutdown sequence
  (flushing I/O, sending a proper goodbye, etc.) would be cleaner.
  (See main.c.)

                      --          --


Makefile.in: Add a 'dist' target

  There is currently no 'dist' Makefile target to produce a release
  tarball.  Adding one requires enumerating the files that belong in
  a distribution.  (See Makefile.in.)

                      --          --

TESTING --------------------------------------------------------------

Torture test

  Something that just keeps running rsync continuously over a data set
  likely to generate problems.

                      --          --


Cross-test versions2001/08/22

  Part of the regression suite should be making sure that we
  don't break backwards compatibility: old clients vs new
  servers and so on.  Ideally we would test both up and down
  from the current release to all old versions.

  Run current rsync versions against significant past releases.

  We might need to omit broken old versions, or versions in which
  particular functionality is broken

  It might be sufficient to test downloads from well-known public
  rsync servers running different versions of rsync.  This will give
  some testing and also be the most common case for having different
  versions and not being able to upgrade.

  Protocol-level compat is partially covered by 'make check29' and
  'make check30', but testing against old binaries is still missing.

                      --          --


Test on kernel source

  Download all versions of kernel; unpack, sync between them.  Also
  sync between uncompressed tarballs.  Compare directories after
  transfer.

  Use local mode; ssh; daemon; --whole-file and --no-whole-file.

  Use awk to pull out the 'speedup' number for each transfer.  Make
  sure it is >= x.

                      --          --


Test large files

  Sparse and non-sparse

                      --          --


Create mutator program for testing

  Insert bytes, delete bytes, swap blocks, ...

                      --          --


Create configure option to enable dangerous tests

                      --          --


Create pipe program for testing

  Create pipe program that makes slow/jerky connections for
  testing Versions of read() and write() that corrupt the
  stream, or abruptly fail

